# Flask-Migrate

This project's database schema is version-controlled with Flask-Migrate
— the `flask db ...` command group. Flask-Migrate is a thin Flask-CLI
wrapper *around* Alembic: under the hood every `flask db` command is
doing exactly what the `alembic` skill describes (diffing your models
against the last-applied revision, writing a versioned migration
script), it's just invoked through Flask instead of the bare `alembic`
CLI, and it's wired to a Flask-SQLAlchemy `db` object rather than a
hand-rolled SQLAlchemy engine. This skill covers that Flask-specific
wrapper layer; see the `alembic` skill for what's happening underneath
(autogenerate diffing, migration file anatomy).

## When to reach for this

- Generating a migration after adding or changing a model in
  `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py`.
- Applying or rolling back migrations, in dev or before deploying.
- Debugging `flask db migrate` reporting "No changes in schema
  detected" when you know the models changed.
- Touching `core/db.py`'s `migrate = Migrate()` / `migrate.init_app(...)`
  wiring, or anything under `migrations/`.

## Quick reference

- **Generate a migration**: `uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db migrate -m "describe your change"`
  — diffs the models against the last-applied revision and writes a new
  file under `migrations/versions/`. Always review the generated file
  before applying it; autogenerate is a diffing heuristic, not a
  guarantee (see the `alembic` skill).
- **Apply migrations**: `uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db upgrade`
  — runs every unapplied revision, in order, up to the latest.
- **Roll back one**: `uv run flask --app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py db downgrade`
  — reverts the single most recently applied revision.
- **No `flask db init` needed here**: most Flask-Migrate tutorials start
  with `flask db init` to scaffold the `migrations/` directory. This
  project's `flask/rest-api` template already ships that directory
  pre-generated (`migrations/env.py`, `alembic.ini`, `script.py.mako`,
  `versions/`) — running `db init` again would fail (the directory
  already exists) and isn't part of this project's workflow. `flask db
  migrate` works immediately, no setup step first.
- **The `--app` flag is load-bearing**: every command above passes
  `--app src/{{ package_name }}/main.py` so Flask can discover
  `create_app()` and build a real app/db before Flask-Migrate can do
  anything — there is no module-level `app = create_app()` for the CLI
  to fall back to (see the `flask` skill's `references/gotchas.md`).
- **Why Flask-SQLAlchemy specifically**: Flask-Migrate's `Migrate` object
  is constructed against a Flask-SQLAlchemy `db = SQLAlchemy()` instance
  (`migrate.init_app(app, db)` in `core/db.py`) — it reads the model
  metadata and engine off that object. This is exactly why Flask's
  `orm=sqlalchemy` (manual SQLAlchemy, no `db` object) uses bare Alembic
  via a separate `migrations-sqlalchemy` layer instead of Flask-Migrate;
  the two ORM choices in this project are not interchangeable here.

